From 291b1e4c7a4518509c0b723e24913765084c7951 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Schneider <marius.schnd@icloud.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:16:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Fetch DASH segments concurrently, join once, and retry a
 failed one
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

download() built its result with `data += chunk` per segment. That is quadratic:
for a hi-res track (~95 MB across 73 segments) it costs 462ms of pure memcpy and
transiently holds two full copies of the track. A single join is 14ms and halves
peak allocation.

Segments are also fetched concurrently now (order preserved via
ThreadPoolExecutor.map) and retried individually. The retry is the real win: one
connection blip previously discarded an otherwise complete 95 MB transfer.

Honest note on the concurrency: on the link measured here it did NOT show a
reliable speedup — throughput variance between runs (39-59s) exceeded the
difference between strategies, and raising workers past 4 changed nothing, so
this transfer is bandwidth-bound rather than round-trip-bound. It is kept
because it cannot be slower and helps on high-latency links, not because a
speedup was demonstrated.

download() had no test coverage upstream; adds 7 tests for ordering, retry,
session lifetime and thread fan-out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
